<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Larsa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Larsa was an ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> that rose to prominence after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>. Under its king Rim-Sin it was one of the main rivals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in the early second millennium BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> conquered Larsa in 1762 BCE, bringing southern Mesopotamia under the Babylonian Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/larsa.jpg" alt="Larsa" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hammurabi%27s_Babylonia_1.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…minor city into a recognised regional player, completing the walls, digging canals, winning modest victories and surviving in a world dominated by Elam to the east, the rival cities of Isin and <b>Larsa</b> to the south, and the Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia under Shamshi-Adad I to the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Afterwards there was modest occupation in the Isin-<b>Larsa</b> and Old Babylonian periods, and then Lagash was largely deserted until a Seleucid fortress was built on the site in the 2nd century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His troops served with Hammurabi of Babylon against the invading Elamites and against <b>Larsa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Isin-<b>Larsa</b> period, when the occupied area reached 140 hectares, the merchant Ea-nasir dealt in copper from Dilmun, shipping ingots that originated in Magan by water to Ur for resale and probably supplying the palace; he was active in the 11th and 19th regnal years of Rim-Sin…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>When that empire collapsed around 2004 BCE, Uruk came under the kings of Isin and then <b>Larsa</b> before Sin-kashid restored its independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When he inherited the throne of his father Sin-Muballit, Babylon held little local sway and the local hegemon was Rim-Sin of <b>Larsa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
